Jack Bolsen wrote:
>
> This statement is great for bouncing around a theory in a newsgroup but
> doesn't stand up as well to real world gaming. First, you'd have to find
> the daggers - wizards aren't generated with them. Granted, they are not
> too hard to find, but in the early game they are much more plentiful in
> the mines than in the main dungeon (varients of daggers like crude
> daggers and elven daggers appear, but I don't count those).

You're starting with an assortment of assumptions here.  Some
valid some not.

The first one is discarding *any* daggers before you have a sizable
quiver of them.  The quality of daggers goes silver, elven, regular,
orcish.  If you ever end up with a quiver of a dozen highly enchanted
silver daggers go ahead and ditch the rest.  Anyways, only start
ditching orcish daggers when your stock of other types gets to
about a dozen.  Unitl then keep them all.  Then when you get that
13th dagger other than orcish ditch one of your orcish ones.  Lather
rinse repeat this process every time you get a silver, elvish or
regular dagger.  Once you have a dozen total of silver, elven and
regular then step up a notch.  Every time you encounter a silver
or elven dagger ditch one regular one.  At some point you'll have
your dozen elven daggers.  I'm using "dozen" here to mean the
number of daggers you've decided to carry in your quiver based on
weight not exactly 12.

The fact that you are ditching the most powerful elven daggers
in favor of the weaker regular ones is your first false assumption
that I notice.

> Second, you would want to determine the b/u/c status of the item in
> question; not hard so long as your pet hasn't perished (as mine did
> early in the game). If your pet has perished then you need an altar.
> Altars don't appear that often. Sometimes you get one before Sokoban but
> not always. I am rather adamant about discovering this for items.

Here's your second false assumption.  Thrown items do not
weld to your hand, so you can even "q"uiver them so you can use
the "f"ire verb to launch them.  Simply quiver your largest stack.
Any throw dagger that slips, try to remember to name it cursed.
You will end up knowing the cursed ones over time.  And again
there is the process of ditching the daggers you don't want.
Combined with ditching in order orcish, regular, elven until you
eventually have quiver of hightly enchanted silver daggers (never
happens except in pudding farming and extinctionist games but
any character by medusa will have a bunch of elven) you can
also ditch the cursed ones of the lower types.  I'll ditch a regular
dagger that slipped knowing it's cursed even before I'll ditch an
orcish dagger that I think isn't cursed.

> Assuming I do Sokoban first and the mines second (which I find gives me
> better chances for survival)

Here's an assumption that I can't label false.  Which order to
do the branches is a matter of taste.  Start doing the mines
before Sokoban and the plentifull daggers will begin to change
your mind about daggers, but there's never need to do the
mines first.

