> So until I get magicbane, I usually do not have sufficient impetus to
> fool with daggers and enhance my ability with them without severely
> limiting my combat chance.

And here's a valid assumption that's a matter of taste.  But you
still seem to be thinking in terms of wielded daggers not thrown
ones.  Until you get Magicbane daggers are often not worth
wielding but daggers are throwing weapon not a wielding one.

My tastes are different in this one way - With a wizard I am
willing to switch my wielded weapon from staff to dagger once
I have either Sting or Magicbane.  I treat Sting as a practice
weapon until Magicbane arrives.  To get Sting I make it from
the first non-stacking elven dagger I get that I am certain is not
cursed.  That takes either a pet stepping on it and it does not
stack when I pick it up or an altar.  And it also means I don't
name Sting until I have at least 3 elven daggers.  But you've
been ditching the more powerfull and inherently rustproof
elven daggers in favor of less powerfull and rustprone regular
daggers so you haven't had the chance to try this.

> Again note that the idea of wizards casting
> spells all the time at everything doesn't work in actual games - but
> sounds good on r.g.r.n.

Wizards start with force bolt.  It's a wand of striking effect.
Very good to use while the manna is available and you are
not hungry yet.  The strategy is 1-2 casts at range when you
first see an advancing monster, then switch to throwing
daggers until you run out of them, then melee if it was nasty
enough to survive that on-slaught (few single monsters are)
or in a big enough crowd (take out the first 5 orcs and the
last 2 are a lot less of a threat).  Deal is, with that strategy
you're exercising 3 different skill slots, all of which you do
want to eventually #enhance.

> >   So give up a shield and doing more damage at range with thrown daggers
> > to do a little more damage at range one with a quarterstaff.

The biggest difference with thrown daggers is as your
skill level increase you throw *more than one per throw*.
Same as how the damage done by your attack spells goes
up with XP levels.

> I'd have to find a shield first. Wizards aren't generated with them and
> they don't grow on trees. Remember, we are talking early/middlegame here.

Another point of taste.  Shields interfere with attack spells.
A couple of attack spells at a distance softens up monsters
so the shield is used far less often and for shorter melee
battles.  But wizards *can* be played combat style.

